  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly cloudy with slow warming through \nSaturday night.\n\nSouthern New England should remain in a cyclonic flow at the\nsurface through Saturday night. A mid level shortwave should\napproach our region Saturday afternoon, before passing overhead\nSaturday night. The question is how much moisture will be\naround. Current thinking is there will be more clouds than not.\nCannot completely dismiss the idea of a stray rain or snow\nshower towards portions of northwest MA, where upslope flow \ninto the Berkshires will also play a role. Eventually, though \nthe lower levels should dry out enough where the risk for \nprecipitation is nearly nil.\n\nRaised low temperatures from the NationalBlend values due to \nexpected cloud cover preventing strong radiational cooling.\nNorth winds become more westerly late tonight into Saturday.\nThat subtle shift should limit cold air advection, and some\nsunshine should contribute to gradually rising temperatures\nearly this weekend. That said, still thinking temperatures\nremain slightly below normal into Sunday.\n\nA Coastal Flood Statement remains in effect for northern facing \nshores of Cape Cod Bay and Nantucket where gusty winds combined \nwith astronomical tides may lead to some minor coastal \nflooding/splashover during the hours directly around high tide \nthis afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Prolonged period of dry weather for most if not\nall of next week with chilly nights and mild days. High temps \nmoderate to above normal levels in the upper 50s to the lower \n60s for the middle and latter half of next week with perhaps a \nday or two reaching into the middle 60s in some spots. \n\nThe long range model guidance is in very good agreement in the\nupper trough shifting east of the region early next week. This\nwill be replaced by upper level ridging/above normal height\nfields. The result will be an extended period of dry weather for \nmost if not all of next week. High temperatures will moderate\nback to above normal levels for the middle to latter portion of \nnext week with highs in the upper 50s to the lower 60s. In fact, \nwould not be surprised to see temperatures overachieve with some\nspots perhaps reaching the middle 60s for a day two. Mainly \nclear skies and light winds will allow for chilly/seasonable \novernight lows in the 20s and 30s.\n\nSo in a nutshell, a dry/tranquil week of weather is in store for \nthe region. A large diurnal spread in temps is also expected\nwith seasonably chilly nights but mild/above normal afternoons.",
